/// [id] with its last two base36 chars bumped to the alphabet successor —
/// ALWAYS a different id that keeps the `sh-<n>-` numeric part, so the
/// near-miss resolution engages. The old `substring(len-2)}zz` collapsed
/// into the EXACT id whenever the minted random tail already ended in zz
/// (~1/36² of starts): red run 39892637833 shard 1/4 (AC1 output, minted
/// id sh-1-hngui7xdrrsfwpzz) — and run 38120494810 shard 3/4 (AC5 stop)
/// before that, papered over per-test by forcing the id. Bumping the two
/// chars closes the hazard in the construction itself.
String _nearMissId(String id) {
  String bump(String c) => c == 'z' ? '0' : _base36[_base36.indexOf(c) + 1];
  return '${id.substring(0, id.length - 2)}'
      '${bump(id[id.length - 2])}${bump(id[id.length - 1])}';
